HairCoaction Internship – Week-wise Report (6 Weeks)
Intern: Pankaj Kumar  |  Role: Frontend + DevOps  |  Project: HairCoaction (Vite + React + Tailwind + Firebase)

Week 1: Foundation, Planning, First Deploy
- Set up project with Vite + React + TypeScript + Tailwind.
- Implemented core Landing Page (src/pages/Landingpage.tsx) with hero, features, pricing, testimonials, CTA, and contact.
- Basic routing scaffold with react-router-dom and app bootstrap (src/App.tsx, src/main.tsx, index.html).
- Initial static deploy (manual) to verify build and hosting readiness.
- Outcome: Working public landing page and baseline build verified.

Week 2: Authentication and Dashboard Structure
- Integrated Firebase auth (email/password + Google) via firebase.ts; created Login and Signup flows.
- Dashboard shell and tabbed navigation patterns in DashboardPage with stubs for sections: Home, Education, Consultation, Event, Community, Profile, Tutorial.
- Built shared components and loader (BeautifulLoader) for consistent UX.
- Outcome: Authenticated area scaffolded; users can register/sign-in and reach dashboard.

Week 3: Feature Modules and API Stubs
- Created feature components and wiring:
  • Education (learning resources)
  • Consultation (appointment-like flow)
  • EventPage (events listing)
  • Community (posts stub)
  • Profile (basic profile view)
- Added Express API stubs under api/ (profile, event, education, consultation, community) with a simple server.ts for future backend integration.
- Outcome: End-to-end flow outlined; future backend endpoints defined for handoff/migration.

Week 4: UX Enhancements and Accessibility
- Dark/Light theming, gradients, animations, and mobile responsiveness across pages.
- Fixed layout polish, keyboard focus states, and smoother transitions.
- Outcome: Production-grade look-and-feel suitable for demos and user testing.

Week 5: Debugging, Code Quality, and CI
- Resolved build break due to merge-conflict artifacts detected in build-output; verified Sidebar.tsx integrity.
- Fixed Landing Page dark mode to be controlled by App (prop-based) eliminating state divergence.
- Standardized scripts and added typecheck.
- Implemented GitHub Actions CI (.github/workflows/ci.yml): npm ci, lint, typecheck, build, and artifact upload.
- Outcome: Repeatable, trusted builds on every push/PR; early error detection.

Week 6: Continuous Deployment and Documentation
- Added Netlify configuration (netlify.toml) and GitHub Actions deploy workflow (.github/workflows/deploy-netlify.yml) for auto-deploys on main.
- Deployment strategy: On push to main → CI → Build → Netlify deploy (requires repo secrets NETLIFY_SITE_ID, NETLIFY_AUTH_TOKEN).
- Authored documentation artifacts (this report + Presentation) for evaluation and future onboarding.
- Outcome: Fully automated CI/CD pipeline with clear docs.

Notable Decisions and Rationale
- Chose Netlify for simple static hosting and frictionless CI/CD for Vite.
- Kept Firebase config for rapid auth enablement; can be moved to env vars later.
- API stubs placed in api/ to enable future migration to a real backend (Node/Express, Supabase functions, or serverless).

How to Operate (Faculty/Reviewer Notes)
- CI: Every push/PR runs lint, typecheck, and build; artifacts are uploaded.
- CD: Merges to main trigger Netlify deploy workflow. Add secrets in GitHub repo settings: NETLIFY_SITE_ID and NETLIFY_AUTH_TOKEN.
- Local: npm ci; npm run dev; npm run build.

Suggestions for Future Work
- Connect a managed DB and auth via Supabase; migrate stubs → real endpoints.
- Add E2E tests (Playwright) and unit tests (Vitest) to CI.
- Introduce Sentry for error monitoring and Semgrep for security scans.
- Containerize for alternative deployment targets if needed.

